Recursion is not required here. Each stack item stores the current string and the counts of opening and closing brackets already used. A closing bracket is pushed only when there is an unmatched opening bracket.

One dry run for `n = 2` starts with `""`, then reaches `"("`. From there it can create `"(("` or `"()"`, and both branches finish as `(())` and `()()`.

The stack stores states that still need to be checked, so this follows the same choices as recursive depth-first search without using the call stack.

```go
func generateParenthesis(n int) []string {
    type state struct {
        text  string
        open  int
        close int
    }

    result := []string{}
    stack := []state{{text: "", open: 0, close: 0}}

    for len(stack) > 0 {
        last := len(stack) - 1
        current := stack[last]
        stack = stack[:last]

        if current.open == n && current.close == n {
            result = append(result, current.text)
            continue
        }

        if current.close < current.open {
            stack = append(stack, state{
                text:  current.text + ")",
                open:  current.open,
                close: current.close + 1,
            })
        }

        if current.open < n {
            stack = append(stack, state{
                text:  current.text + "(",
                open:  current.open + 1,
                close: current.close,
            })
        }
    }

    return result
}
```

Time complexity: `O(Cn * n)`, because all `Cn` valid strings of length `2n` must be produced.

Space complexity: `O(Cn * n)` including the result and pending stack states. The extra state for one path is `O(n)`.

This version does not try to prune early. It fills all `2 * n` positions with either bracket and validates each complete string afterward. That makes the code easy to compare with the problem rules, although it does extra work.

Dry run for `n = 1`:

- `(` followed by `(` gives `((`, which fails because the balance is not zero.
- `(` followed by `)` gives `()`, which is stored.
- Strings starting with `)` are rejected as soon as the balance goes below zero.

```cpp
class Solution {
    bool isValid(const string& text) {
        int balance = 0;
        for (char ch : text) {
            if (ch == '(') {
                ++balance;
            } else {
                --balance;
            }
            if (balance < 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return balance == 0;
    }

    void build(string& current, int n, vector<string>& result) {
        if (current.size() == static_cast<size_t>(2 * n)) {
            if (isValid(current)) {
                result.push_back(current);
            }
            return;
        }

        current.push_back('(');
        build(current, n, result);
        current.pop_back();

        current.push_back(')');
        build(current, n, result);
        current.pop_back();
    }

public:
    vector<string> generateParenthesis(int n) {
        vector<string> result;
        string current;
        build(current, n, result);
        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(2^(2n) * n)` for generating and checking all possible strings.

Space complexity: `O(n)` extra space for the current string and recursion stack, excluding the output.

The list for `pairs` pairs can be made from smaller lists. Put one new pair around a result with `inside` pairs, then place a result with the remaining pairs after it.

For example, when building three pairs, the first pair can contain 0, 1, or 2 pairs. Combining every choice gives all results without duplicates.

The empty result at `dp[0]` is important. It lets the first pair be formed as `"()"`.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<String> generateParenthesis(int n) {
        List<List<String>> dp = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            dp.add(new ArrayList<>());
        }
        dp.get(0).add("");

        for (int pairs = 1; pairs <= n; pairs++) {
            List<String> current = dp.get(pairs);

            for (int inside = 0; inside < pairs; inside++) {
                int outside = pairs - 1 - inside;

                for (String left : dp.get(inside)) {
                    for (String right : dp.get(outside)) {
                        current.add("(" + left + ")" + right);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return dp.get(n);
    }
}
```

For the edge case `n = 0`, the method returns a list containing the empty string, which represents one valid arrangement of zero pairs.

Time complexity: `O(Cn * n)` in output terms, where `Cn` is the number of valid combinations. The intermediate lists also take time to build.

Space complexity: `O(Cn * n)` including the stored results and intermediate dynamic programming lists.

This version tracks how many opening and closing parentheses are still available instead of counting how many have been used. A closing parenthesis is allowed when `closeLeft > openLeft`, because that means an opening parenthesis is waiting to be closed.

The helper uses one mutable `StringBuilder`. After each recursive call, the added character is removed so the next choice starts from the correct string.

For the edge cases `n = 0` and `n = 1`, it returns `""` and `"()"` respectively. It also never creates a string beginning with `)`.

```csharp
class Solution
{
    private void Build(List<string> result, StringBuilder current, int openLeft, int closeLeft)
    {
        if (openLeft == 0 && closeLeft == 0)
        {
            result.Add(current.ToString());
            return;
        }

        if (openLeft > 0)
        {
            current.Append('(');
            Build(result, current, openLeft - 1, closeLeft);
            current.Length--;
        }

        if (closeLeft > openLeft)
        {
            current.Append(')');
            Build(result, current, openLeft, closeLeft - 1);
            current.Length--;
        }
    }

    public List<string> GenerateParenthesis(int n)
    {
        List<string> result = new List<string>();
        StringBuilder current = new StringBuilder();
        Build(result, current, n, n);
        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(Cn * n)`, where `Cn` is the number of valid strings and each result has length `2n`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` auxiliary space for the builder and recursion depth, excluding the returned results.

